--TEST--
the registry is the whole roster of `Core`, so a member it does not hold is `E0405` wherever it is written — on an instance, on a class it states, and on a class it has never heard of (ADR 0008, ADR 0061)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A registered class checked like any other: `Core\Uuid` states no `version`,
// and an instance is where its members are reached from. This used to type-check
// and then panic in `nvs-ir`, which had a call with no resolved target to lower.
Core\Uuid $id = Core\Uuid::v4();
echo $id->version(), "\n";
// The static half of the same question, for a class the registry does state.
echo Core\Uuid::variant($id), "\n";
// And a class it does not: `Core\Env` is a name `nvs_hir` resolves because
// nothing under `Core\` needs a declaration, which used to make every member of
// it trusted. The registry is the roster, so `EOL` is knowably wrong here —
// `docs/spec/02-php-migration.md` maps `PHP_EOL`, and mapping it is not shipping
// it.
echo Core\Env::EOL, "\n";
echo Core\Log::info("started"), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Uuid` has no method named `version`
%A
error[E0405]: `Core\Uuid` has no member named `variant`
%A
error[E0405]: `Core\Env` has no constant named `EOL`
%A
error[E0405]: `Core\Log` has no member named `info`
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
